WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.629
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level C one.

2
00:00:05.929 --> 00:00:09.571
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.871 --> 00:00:17.074
Unit eighteen: Advanced Reported Speech Nuances. Today you will decide when to backshift and which reporting verb to use.

4
00:00:17.374 --> 00:00:20.073
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.033 --> 00:00:24.313
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:26.093
Backshift.

7
00:00:27.083 --> 00:00:29.965
Backshift means moving a tense one step back.

8
00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:32.538
Backshift is common in reports.

9
00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:35.837
Your turn. Say: Backshift.

10
00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:39.736
Backshift.

11
00:00:40.483 --> 00:00:41.751
No backshift.

12
00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:45.574
No backshift means keeping the original tense.

13
00:00:46.183 --> 00:00:48.473
No backshift for facts still true.

14
00:00:49.083 --> 00:00:52.072
Your turn. Say: No backshift.

15
00:00:54.933 --> 00:00:56.294
No backshift.

16
00:00:57.050 --> 00:00:58.620
Reporting verb choice.

17
00:00:59.617 --> 00:01:03.033
Reporting verb choice means selecting the best verb for meaning.

18
00:01:03.617 --> 00:01:06.034
Reporting verb choice shows attitude.

19
00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:09.906
Your turn. Say: Reporting verb choice.

20
00:01:12.767 --> 00:01:14.627
Reporting verb choice.

21
00:01:15.383 --> 00:01:16.501
Insist.

22
00:01:17.517 --> 00:01:19.493
Insist means to say firmly.

23
00:01:20.083 --> 00:01:21.584
She will insist on it.

24
00:01:22.183 --> 00:01:24.789
Your turn. Say: Insist.

25
00:01:27.633 --> 00:01:28.878
Insist.

26
00:01:29.617 --> 00:01:30.304
Claim.

27
00:01:31.317 --> 00:01:33.990
Claim means to say something is true without proof.

28
00:01:34.583 --> 00:01:36.037
They claim it is safe.

29
00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:38.931
Your turn. Say: Claim.

30
00:01:41.767 --> 00:01:42.640
Claim.

31
00:01:43.383 --> 00:01:44.907
Modality shift.

32
00:01:45.917 --> 00:01:49.611
Modality shift means a change of modal in reported speech.

33
00:01:50.217 --> 00:01:52.634
A modality shift: will to would.

34
00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:56.227
Your turn. Say: Modality shift.

35
00:01:59.067 --> 00:02:00.346
Modality shift.

36
00:02:01.083 --> 00:02:02.142
Nuanced meaning.

37
00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:05.277
Nuanced meaning means a subtle meaning.

38
00:02:05.883 --> 00:02:07.117
A nuanced meaning.

39
00:02:07.717 --> 00:02:10.497
Your turn. Say: Nuanced meaning.

40
00:02:13.333 --> 00:02:14.625
Nuanced meaning.

41
00:02:15.383 --> 00:02:16.501
Tone of reporting.

42
00:02:17.517 --> 00:02:20.201
Tone of reporting means the attitude of the reporter.

43
00:02:20.817 --> 00:02:22.305
The tone of reporting.

44
00:02:22.917 --> 00:02:25.882
Your turn. Say: Tone of reporting.

45
00:02:28.733 --> 00:02:30.001
Tone of reporting.

46
00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:34.567
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:35.417 --> 00:02:39.274
Visal, if she said 'The museum opens at nine', do I have to backshift?

48
00:02:40.383 --> 00:02:51.374
Not always. She said the museum opens at nine is fine if it is still true. Backshift is optional when the fact is general or the report is made soon after.

49
00:02:52.483 --> 00:02:54.111
When is backshift required?

50
00:02:55.217 --> 00:03:07.714
When the situation has changed or the report is distant: He said he was leaving, and he did. Modals shift too: will becomes would, can becomes could, may becomes might.

51
00:03:08.817 --> 00:03:10.874
How does the reporting verb help?

52
00:03:11.983 --> 00:03:27.189
It carries tone: insisted, claimed, admitted, warned. Choose the verb for the meaning, not only said. A warned that a storm was coming is clearer than said, because it also shows purpose.

53
00:03:28.333 --> 00:03:29.810
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:37.491
Grammar focus: Reported speech: reporting verb plus (backshifted) clause; no backshift for permanent facts.

55
00:03:37.791 --> 00:03:39.082
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:39.783 --> 00:03:41.957
She said that the museum opens at nine.

57
00:03:42.850 --> 00:03:44.501
He said that he was leaving.

58
00:03:45.417 --> 00:03:47.207
They warned that a storm would arrive.

59
00:03:48.167 --> 00:03:55.022
Backshift when the situation has changed, but not for facts that are still true. Pick reporting verbs for tone and purpose.

60
00:03:55.783 --> 00:03:58.900
Be careful! Don't say: He said that he is leaving.

61
00:04:00.867 --> 00:04:02.843
Say: He said that he was leaving.

62
00:04:03.143 --> 00:04:04.562
Backshift the tense.

63
00:04:05.367 --> 00:04:07.961
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:08.767 --> 00:04:10.464
To Shift or Not to Shift.

65
00:04:10.764 --> 00:04:13.577
She said that the museum opens at nine every day.

66
00:04:14.567 --> 00:04:17.762
He said that he was leaving, and he left the next morning.

67
00:04:18.767 --> 00:04:21.126
They warned that a storm would arrive by evening.

68
00:04:22.133 --> 00:04:24.771
The guide insisted that visitors keep together.

69
00:04:25.767 --> 00:04:28.974
The reporting verb tells us how to understand the words.

70
00:04:30.067 --> 00:04:34.173
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:35.017 --> 00:04:37.623
He said he blank leaving. (backshifted)

72
00:04:37.923 --> 00:04:40.961
A, was. B, is. C, will be.

73
00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:46.372
The answer is A: was.

74
00:04:47.517 --> 00:04:50.561
will in direct speech becomes blank in reported speech.

75
00:04:50.861 --> 00:04:54.062
A, must. B, would. C, can.

76
00:04:57.600 --> 00:04:59.333
The answer is B: would.

77
00:05:00.483 --> 00:05:02.912
Backshift is optional when the fact is blank.

78
00:05:03.212 --> 00:05:07.133
A, still true. B, false. C, unknown.

79
00:05:10.667 --> 00:05:12.817
The answer is A: still true.

80
00:05:13.983 --> 00:05:16.111
Which verb shows a warning purpose?

81
00:05:16.411 --> 00:05:19.437
A, told. B, warned. C, said.

82
00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:24.837
The answer is B: warned.

83
00:05:25.983 --> 00:05:29.132
According to the reading, what did the guide insist?

84
00:05:29.432 --> 00:05:34.897
A, visitors keep together. B, the museum is closed. C, the storm is over.

85
00:05:38.433 --> 00:05:41.246
The answer is A: visitors keep together.

86
00:05:42.433 --> 00:05:45.805
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:50.809
Report a statement using a nuanced reporting verb like 'insist' or 'claim'.

88
00:05:59.617 --> 00:06:01.059
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:01.359 --> 00:06:10.922
'I did not break the rule,' he said. He claimed that he had not broken the rule. I chose claimed because it shows that I am not sure the statement is true.

90
00:06:11.222 --> 00:06:12.861
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:13.750 --> 00:06:22.649
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and reported speech: reporting verb plus (backshifted) clause; no backshift for permanent facts.

92
00:06:22.949 --> 00:06:27.366
Today's value is truthfulness. Tell the truth and check the facts.

93
00:06:27.666 --> 00:06:30.388
See you in Unit nineteen. Goodbye!

94
00:06:30.688 --> 00:06:33.851
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

